Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~ says:
> Perry,
> 
> Let's get down to it. I'll step back in to hopefully simplify things. I
> judge any money-making endeavor in relative terms, as well they should be.
> Thus, if it isn't outpacing inflation, interest rates, mutual funds, or
> other low-risk means of income, it just isn't worth it. I'm a proponent of
> intelligent trading, but I put the following to you. Figuring the following
> factors, if you can say you're making 25+% net returns, then I think it
> is time to stop talking, and start giving specific advice (your choice as
> to whether you should sell it or not) :-)

I've had periods where my investment income has exceeded my salary
income, and periods where I've done badly. Overall, I've had
respectable returns -- I won't say how respectable. I have a pretty
strong rule, however, which is never to give anything whatsoever other
than general advice.

> 1. Transaction fees

If you want to minimise transaction fees, use a discount or deep
discount broker for stock transactions, and make as few transactions
that involve brokerage fees as possible, meaning buy for the long
haul, and use no-load mutual funds when it seems appropriate.  People
seem to be making a lot more fees than seems rational -- they are
important if you are trading every day, not if you are buying for the
long haul.

> 3. Human resources (your time)

This is quite cheap for most people. Yeah, I know the usual economic
argument. "I get paid N dollars an hour, so that means that five hours
spent on research cost me 5N dollars." All fine and well -- if you
actually spent the time doing research instead of paid work. Did you?
If not, your time was not worth 5N dollars. It was probably worth very
little, in fact.

As an example, lets say that you make $30 an hour. Lets say that you
argue to yourself "well, making my own dinner every day takes me an
hour, so that means it costs me $30, so I can go out and spend $30 on
buying dinner instead and not feel bad." Do that, and the end of the
month will find you $900 poorer, no matter what your silly thoughts on
the matter might be.

> 4. Losses (one's successes cannot be put into perspective until you subtract
>    from then one's failures.)

You have to be prepared for the fact that you will sometimes lose
money. Just try to make sure you don't do it that often, and never be
tied to a position -- in particular, don't hold something just in the
hopes that it might rise above where you bought it someday -- consider
your position fresh every time you look at it. If it doesn't look like
something will rise, don't hold it just to make yourself feel like
less of a loser.

> Once you account for all that (and I hope I haven't missed anything), then
> compare it with other forms of investment (including similar factors in
> those).

There is one "riskless" form of investment, which is the money market
fund. Money market funds are currently paying negative real interest.

I'm not saying you have to put your money in anything in particular.
There is nothing wrong with equity and other similar mutual funds.  I
think, however, you are something of a fool if you consider the notion
of having your money lose its value every year to be "safe".

Perry

