Date: Tue, 10 Aug 1993 11:10:38 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: What's Perry's Notion of Religion? 

starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu says:
> >I said nothing about theistic cosmologies. Its possible to be
> >religious without involving a "god". 
> >
> >In fact, I'd say that that quoted passage more or less says what I've
> >said the natural law says: that there is an absolute right ans wrong,
> >that it is part of the rules of the universe, etc.
> 
> Very well, Perry, I'll bite: what is your stipulated meaning for the
> terms "religious"?

Unreasoning belief in a revealed idea system. Whether that idea system
is "you must worship Mumbo Jumbo" or "you must sacrifice yourself for
the good of all" or "the non-coercion principle is the definition of
the Good" matters little.

> You've placed this in opposition to reason.

By definition.

> Please let me in on how natural law is "religious" in any sense
> which entails unreason.

It tends to hold that there are immutable categories that we call "The
Good" and "The Bad" into which all actions fall and that there are a
set of "Inalienable Rights" that one has, much like platonic essences,
whic are defined strictly and clearly, the violation of which is
"Bad".

This viewpoint holds that the source of "The Good" and "The Bad" is
the set of rules. Where do the rules come from? "The nature of
things", i.e. they are revealed truth, whether or not that revelation
id done by god or not. There is no reason involved. No one asks where
"the good" and "the bad" really come from -- although some noises are
made to that effect, just as Aquinas made noises to the effect of
proving god by logical means.

Now, there is another viewpoint, which is this: we define the good and
the bad based simply on our own goals, which are arational.
Explicable by reason, but arational. It is no more rational to want to
survive than not to want to survive, but because of evolution one
would expect most of the creatures you would observe in the world
would have the goal of surviving, and in fact we do. Based on these
arational, but perfectly explicable, desires, we construct for
ourselves categories of the good and the bad, based on what we have
found will tend to promote or fail to promote our selected goals. Note
that there is no question of "Good" and "Bad" here being absolute --
we recognise that we have defined those categories based on what we
think will promote or fail to promote our goals, and those categories
may be changed as our information changes and as our goals change. In
other words, there is no "law" here, only that which we have made.

> I suspect that the absolute notion of morality you object to is more
> specifically the notion of categorical obligation, but you haven't
> confirmed this.

I object to any notion of absolute good and bad. Personal, selected
morality I have no trouble with -- its basically a shorthand way of
saying "what I think will promote what I believe to be good FOR MY
GOALS." However, it has very little to do with what other people would
call morality.

Perry

