Derek Zahn:
> Yes, I think that Nick's concerns about transaction costs are based
> on a vision of the "little guy" making many transactions, playing
> with bumps in the market like institutional investors can. 

No, I'm basing it on the fact that many indy investors ignore important
transaction costs and don't do the tedious but critical arithmetic, and 
it would pain me to see an Extropian piss away their cryonics seed money, 
so here goes another naive altruistic attempt to knock some sense into 
this discussion.  Commissions are only the obvious transaction costs.  
(Well, taxes should be obvious too, but they're hard to compute so many 
people skip it, too their great detriment when the highway robbers come 
calling).  

Many brokers don't have good floor traders and get the 
wrong side of bid/ask spreads on thinly traded stocks.  On the smaller 
high-tech firms folks in this group might have an "edge" in (or think 
they do) that can cost you 1% coming and going, or 2% on _one investment_!
As I pointed out losing 1.5% per year can reduce your average return 
over 100 years by over a factor of 4.  Over the long haul, these 
seemingly trivial and/or easily ignored transaction costs dominate your 
costs.  The information you have must be _unique_ to
you or a very tiny number of investors, and it must be _important_
enough that its impact on the stock price will beat the transaction
costs by a reasonable margin.  If you haven't studied the company's
finances in detail (this often involves more than just looking at
the annual report) and tracked the consequences of your unique
information, you're shooting in the dark.  Most individual investors
mistake their dime-a-dozen opinion for unique and important
information.  Finally, you have to realize that no matter how
unique and important your information, there are other things you
don't know, random noise that will dwarf the impact what you
have discovered.  You are gambling (but, if you have that information, 
with a slight edge on the house instead of vice versa).

